      "name": null,
      "problem": "Orlando used $0.3\\,\\text{kg}$ of powdered fertilizer on Monday morning. He then used $0.18\\,\\text{kg}$ of powdered fertilizer on Monday afternoon. How much powder did Orlando use that day?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Expressed as a decimal, Orlando used$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{kg}$ of powdered fertilizer.",
      "worked_solution": "To determine the amount of powder Orlando used, we need to calculate the sum $0.3+0.18.$\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $0.3$ and $2$ decimal places in $0.18.$\n\nTo add the numbers, they both need to have the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0.3=0.30\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Orlando used $0.48\\,\\text{kg}$ of powdered fertilizer.",
