    "q-166808": {
      "dom_id": "question-166808",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "During a particular weekend, Orlando made $10\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ liters of liquid fertilizer. He used $9\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ liters from this amount. How much liquid fertilizer did Orlando have left over? Express your answer as a mixed number in its lowest terms.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Orlando had$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$liters of liquid fertilizer left over.",
      "worked_solution": "Orlando had $(10\\,\\frac{1}{2}-9\\,\\frac{1}{6})$ liters of liquid fertilizer left over.\n\nWriting $10\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n10\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{10\\times 2+1}{2}=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$\n\nWriting $9\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n9\\,\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{9\\times 6+1}{6}=\\frac{55}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{55}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $6.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{21}{2}$ over a denominator of $6,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}=\\frac{21\\times 3}{2\\times 3}=\\frac{63}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{63}{6}-\\frac{55}{6}=\\frac{8}{6}\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{6}=\\frac{8÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Orlando had $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ liters of liquid fertilizer left over.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
